-- Q3673: Security Controls and Monitoring [medium/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Extend log retention to one year using tiered storage to balance cost and compliance","30 days of log retention is sufficient for all regulated organizations","Delete logs immediately after review to minimize storage expenditures","Regulatory requirements for log retention periods do not currently exist"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-92, Section 4.2, log retention periods must meet regulatory requirements. When regulations mandate one-year retention, a tiered storage approach (hot for recent logs, cold for older logs) balances compliance with cost efficiency.
Wrong (B): A 30-day retention period fails to meet the one-year regulatory mandate and would leave the organization non-compliant.
Wrong (C): Deleting logs immediately eliminates the forensic and audit trail needed for investigations and regulatory compliance.
Wrong (D): Multiple regulations (PCI DSS, HIPAA, SOX) impose specific log retention requirements on covered organizations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3673;

-- Q3675: Security Controls and Monitoring [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Privilege escalation through intentional exploitation of system vulnerabilities","Denial of access caused by overly restrictive permission policies","Unchecked growth in the number of roles defined within the IAM system","Privilege creep from accumulating unnecessary permissions across role changes"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5, control AC-2, organizations must periodically review accounts to ensure access rights remain appropriate. Privilege creep occurs when users retain permissions from prior roles, violating least privilege.
Wrong (A): Privilege escalation is an intentional attack technique, not the gradual accumulation described in this scenario.
Wrong (B): Access denial is the opposite problem — restricting legitimate access rather than allowing excessive access.
Wrong (C): Role explosion refers to proliferation of role definitions in RBAC systems, not individual user permission accumulation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3675;

-- Q3677: Security Controls and Monitoring [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Hybrid encryption is mandated by all current email transmission standards","Symmetric encryption handles bulk data efficiently while asymmetric encryption solves key distribution securely","Asymmetric encryption lacks the capability to encrypt message data directly","Using both methods together provides no meaningful advantage over a single method"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-175B, the hybrid approach uses AES for fast bulk encryption and RSA to securely distribute the AES session key without pre-shared secrets. This pattern underpins TLS, PGP, and S/MIME.
Wrong (A): No standard mandates this specific approach; it is an engineering best practice chosen for performance and security reasons.
Wrong (C): Asymmetric algorithms like RSA can encrypt data, but they are computationally impractical for large payloads.
Wrong (D): The hybrid model provides clear advantages — performance from symmetric encryption and secure key exchange from asymmetric encryption.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3677;

-- Q3680: Security Controls and Monitoring [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Service accounts carry no meaningful security risk to the organization","Service accounts hold elevated privileges, persist beyond employee departures, and create unmonitored attack paths","Service accounts are automatically hardened and secured by the host operating system","Periodic access reviews are required only for interactive human user accounts"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5, control AC-2, all account types including service accounts must be periodically reviewed. Service accounts often hold elevated privileges, run continuously, and survive personnel changes, creating unmonitored pathways for unauthorized access.
Wrong (A): Service accounts pose significant risk precisely because they often have broad privileges and are not tied to individual identity lifecycle events.
Wrong (C): Operating systems do not automatically secure service accounts; they require deliberate configuration and monitoring.
Wrong (D): NIST AC-2 explicitly requires review of all account types, not just human user accounts.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3680;

-- Q3683: Incident Response and Recovery [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Analyzing malware samples collected from previously reported incidents","Notifying law enforcement agencies about anticipated future security incidents","Forming the IR team, deploying detection tools, and building response playbooks","Purchasing comprehensive cyber insurance coverage for the institution"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, Section 2.1, preparation is the foundation of incident response. Key activities include establishing the IR team, deploying monitoring tools, creating playbooks, and conducting exercises to build readiness.
Wrong (A): Malware analysis occurs during the detection and analysis phase, not the preparation phase.
Wrong (B): Proactively notifying law enforcement of hypothetical future incidents is not a standard preparation activity under the NIST framework.
Wrong (D): While cyber insurance may support risk management, it is not the primary focus of the NIST preparation phase.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3683;

-- Q3684: Incident Response and Recovery [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Preparation phase focusing on readiness planning","Containment phase limiting damage spread","Post-incident activity documenting lessons learned","Detection and analysis phase confirming the incident"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, detection and analysis involves identifying potential incidents through alerts, correlating evidence from multiple sources, and confirming whether an actual breach has occurred. Reviewing SIEM alerts and firewall logs fits this phase.
Wrong (A): Preparation occurs before incidents and focuses on building IR capability, not investigating active alerts.
Wrong (B): Containment limits damage after an incident is confirmed, which has not yet happened at this stage.
Wrong (C): Post-incident activity occurs after resolution and focuses on documenting findings and improving processes.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3684;

-- Q3685: Incident Response and Recovery [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Pay the ransom demand to restore encrypted files as quickly as possible","Reformat all network-connected systems immediately to eliminate the malware","Isolate the infected workstation from the network to prevent lateral spread","Notify external media outlets about the ransomware breach immediately"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, Section 3.3, the first containment priority is preventing spread. Isolating the infected workstation stops ransomware from propagating to file shares and other systems on the network.
Wrong (A): Paying ransom is not recommended by NIST or law enforcement, as it funds criminal activity and does not guarantee recovery.
Wrong (B): Reformatting the entire network is excessive and premature; containment should be targeted to affected systems first.
Wrong (D): Media notification is a communications step handled later in the response process, not an immediate containment action.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3685;

-- Q3687: Incident Response and Recovery [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Restoring systems from verified clean backups and returning them to production with monitoring","Identifying the initial attack vector that enabled the ransomware infection","Conducting structured interviews with employees who observed the incident","Drafting the final post-incident report for executive leadership review"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, Section 3.3, recovery involves restoring systems to normal operation through verified backups, system rebuilds, and integrity validation before returning services to production with enhanced monitoring.
Wrong (B): Identifying the initial attack vector is part of detection/analysis or post-incident activity, not the recovery phase.
Wrong (C): Employee interviews support the investigation but are not a recovery-phase activity focused on restoring systems.
Wrong (D): The final incident report is a post-incident activity that occurs after systems are fully restored.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3687;

-- Q3690: Incident Response and Recovery [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A forensic image captures all data including deleted files and slack space, unlike a standard backup","A forensic image compresses data more efficiently, producing smaller output than a standard backup","A standard backup includes application configurations that a forensic image inherently excludes","A forensic image and standard backup are functionally identical, making the choice procedural only"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-86, Section 4.2, a forensic image is a bit-for-bit copy of the entire storage device, preserving deleted files, slack space, and unallocated areas. This completeness is essential for thorough forensic analysis and legal admissibility.
Wrong (B): Forensic images are typically larger than standard backups because they capture all disk sectors, not just active files.
Wrong (C): Standard backups copy active files and configurations but miss deleted files and unallocated space that forensic analysis requires.
Wrong (D): They are fundamentally different — forensic images capture the complete disk state while backups capture only active data.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3690;

-- Q3693: Incident Response and Recovery [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No classification is needed since the vendor deleted the email without accessing the data","A data breach requiring notification analysis because PII left organizational control regardless of access","A minor policy violation warranting only an internal HR reprimand for the sender","A near-miss event to be logged for training purposes with no formal response required"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, unauthorized disclosure of PII to an external party constitutes a breach regardless of intent. The transmission of 450 Social Security numbers outside organizational control triggers notification analysis under most state breach notification laws.
Wrong (A): The vendor''s deletion does not negate the breach — the data left organizational control, which itself constitutes the reportable event.
Wrong (C): While the employee may face HR consequences, the incident''s classification depends on the data exposure, not the employee''s intent.
Wrong (D): An actual disclosure of SSNs to an external party exceeds a near-miss and requires formal incident response procedures.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3693;

-- Q3694: Incident Response and Recovery [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Severity 1 (critical) — confirmed C2 communication from a server holding 80,000 customer records","Severity 4 (informational) — the 200 KB transfer volume is too small for meaningful exfiltration","Severity 3 (low) — the traffic occurred outside business hours with no active users on the system","Severity 2 (medium) — the threat intelligence match may be a false positive requiring normal-hours review"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, Severity 1 applies when confirmed compromise threatens highly sensitive data. Command-and-control communication from a server with 80,000 customer records indicates active compromise requiring immediate response regardless of traffic volume.
Wrong (B): Even small C2 beacons signal attacker presence and potential for escalation; data volume alone does not determine severity.
Wrong (C): The time of occurrence is irrelevant to severity when confirmed C2 communication indicates an active compromise of sensitive systems.
Wrong (D): A confirmed threat intelligence match to a known C2 server warrants immediate critical response, not deferred investigation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3694;

-- Q3695: Incident Response and Recovery [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["To reduce the organization''s marketing expenses during a breach event","To ensure external experts are available without delay when a crisis occurs","To permanently outsource all security monitoring to third-party providers","To eliminate the need for maintaining an internal incident response team"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-61, Section 2.3.4, pre-established relationships with law enforcement, forensic firms, and legal counsel ensure these resources are available immediately during a crisis. Assembling contacts during an active incident causes critical delays.
Wrong (A): External IR relationships serve response readiness, not marketing cost reduction, which is unrelated to incident preparedness.
Wrong (C): These relationships supplement internal capabilities for crisis situations rather than replacing ongoing security monitoring.
Wrong (D): External partnerships are intended to augment the internal IR team, not replace it; the internal team retains primary response responsibility.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3695;

-- Q3696: Incident Response and Recovery [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["To measure network throughput and bandwidth under simulated stress conditions","To test the speed of backup restoration procedures across critical systems","To validate the IR plan through discussion, testing roles and decision-making processes","To evaluate individual employee productivity metrics during incident scenarios"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-61, Section 3.1.2, tabletop exercises are discussion-based simulations where the IR team walks through a scenario to test plan effectiveness, clarify roles, and identify communication gaps without impacting production systems.
Wrong (A): Measuring network throughput is a technical performance test, not a discussion-based exercise focused on procedural readiness.
Wrong (B): Testing backup restoration speed is a technical drill, distinct from the discussion-based format of a tabletop exercise.
Wrong (D): Tabletop exercises evaluate team coordination and IR plan effectiveness, not individual employee productivity metrics.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3696;

-- Q3697: Incident Response and Recovery [hard/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both metrics fall within accepted industry best practices for incident response","Both MTTD and MTTR are critically high, giving attackers a 75-day window for escalation","Only the 30-day MTTR is concerning while the 45-day MTTD is acceptable","These metrics are not meaningful indicators for evaluating security program effectiveness"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, Section 3.1, minimizing detection and response times is critical. A 45-day MTTD allows extensive lateral movement and exfiltration, and the combined 75-day timeline dramatically increases breach scope and cost.
Wrong (A): Industry leaders target detection in hours to days; 45 days MTTD is far outside best practice for any organization.
Wrong (C): Both metrics are concerning — the 45-day MTTD gives attackers substantial undetected dwell time before response even begins.
Wrong (D): MTTD and MTTR are widely recognized as key performance indicators that directly correlate with breach severity and cost.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3697;

-- Q3698: Incident Response and Recovery [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Restoring affected systems to their normal pre-incident operational state","Calculating the total financial impact and losses caused by the incident","Notifying customers and regulators about the data compromise event","Eliminating all malware, backdoors, and unauthorized access from the environment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, Section 3.3, eradication focuses on completely removing the threat — eliminating malware, closing backdoors, removing unauthorized accounts, and patching exploited vulnerabilities to prevent re-compromise.
Wrong (A): Restoring systems to normal operation is the objective of the recovery phase, which follows eradication.
Wrong (B): Calculating financial impact is a post-incident activity, not part of the eradication phase''s threat removal focus.
Wrong (C): Customer notification is a communication and compliance activity handled separately from technical eradication efforts.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3698;

-- Q3699: Incident Response and Recovery [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Comparing the file sizes of individual files between the original and the copy","Visually inspecting a sample of file contents for obvious differences","Checking file creation timestamps to confirm they match the original drive","Generating and comparing cryptographic hash values like SHA-256 for both copies"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-86, Section 4.3, cryptographic hash values such as SHA-256 produce a unique fingerprint for the entire disk image. Any alteration, even a single bit, produces a completely different hash, providing mathematically provable integrity for legal proceedings.
Wrong (A): File size comparison cannot detect bit-level modifications within files that preserve overall size.
Wrong (B): Visual inspection is impractical for large data volumes and cannot detect subtle changes in binary data.
Wrong (C): File timestamps can be manipulated and do not verify the actual content integrity of the forensic image.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3699;

-- Q3701: Incident Response and Recovery [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Contents of RAM that are lost when the system powers off","Active network connections that terminate upon system shutdown","Running processes that cease to exist when the system is powered down","Files stored on the hard drive that persist after system shutdown"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-86, Section 4.1, non-volatile evidence persists after system shutdown. Files stored on hard drives remain intact and recoverable through forensic imaging regardless of power state.
Wrong (A): RAM contents are volatile — they are lost immediately when the system loses power or is shut down.
Wrong (B): Active network connections are volatile and terminate when the system is disconnected or powered off.
Wrong (C): Running processes exist only in memory and cease when the system shuts down, making them volatile evidence.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3701;

-- Q3702: Incident Response and Recovery [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["There is no meaningful difference between the two exercise formats","A tabletop exercise is more rigorous because it tests strategic decision-making","A full-scale simulation tests hands-on technical execution while a tabletop is discussion-based","Full-scale simulations are restricted to military and government organizations only"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, Section 3.5, full-scale simulations involve executing actual technical response actions — isolating systems, conducting forensics, deploying countermeasures — in a realistic environment, validating both procedural and technical capabilities.
Wrong (A): The two formats differ significantly in execution method, resource requirements, and the types of gaps they reveal.
Wrong (B): Tabletop exercises test decision-making through discussion but lack the hands-on technical rigor of full-scale simulations.
Wrong (D): Full-scale simulations are recommended for any organization seeking to validate technical IR capabilities, not just military entities.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3702;

-- Q3703: Incident Response and Recovery [hard/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An attack timeline reconstructing the full intrusion from initial access to detection","A chain of custody log documenting evidence handling and transfers","A backup restoration log tracking system recovery procedures and outcomes","A change management record of authorized modifications to production systems"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, Section 3.4, the attack timeline (kill chain reconstruction) maps the full attacker sequence — from initial phishing compromise through lateral movement, privilege escalation, and data access — to identify all affected systems and security gaps.
Wrong (B): A chain of custody log tracks evidence handling procedures, not the chronological sequence of attacker activities.
Wrong (C): A backup restoration log documents recovery actions, not the attacker''s progression through the environment.
Wrong (D): A change management record tracks authorized system changes, not unauthorized attacker activities.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3703;

-- Q3705: Incident Response and Recovery [hard/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Conducting more frequent vulnerability scans to identify new exposures earlier","Replacing the web server software with an alternative vendor''s product entirely","Terminating the employee who was responsible for applying the missed patch","Strengthening the vulnerability management program with defined remediation SLAs and accountability"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, Section 3.4, lessons-learned reports should address root causes and recommend systemic improvements. The root cause was process failure, not the vulnerability itself, so the recommendation targets remediation SLAs and accountability per NIST SP 800-40 Rev. 4.
Wrong (A): More frequent scanning does not address the core issue — the vulnerability was already identified but never remediated within six months.
Wrong (B): Changing vendors does not fix the underlying patch management process failure that would affect any software platform.
Wrong (C): Terminating an individual does not address the systemic process gap that allowed a critical finding to go unremediated.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3705;

-- Q3706: Incident Response and Recovery [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Performing all technical forensic analysis and evidence collection personally","Handling all external media inquiries and public communications personally","Coordinating the response effort, making key decisions, and serving as communication hub","Drafting the final post-incident report and distributing it to stakeholders"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, Section 2.4, the incident commander coordinates activities across technical, legal, and management teams. The commander makes key containment and escalation decisions while serving as the central communication point.
Wrong (A): Technical forensic analysis is delegated to specialized team members, not performed personally by the incident commander.
Wrong (B): Media inquiries are typically handled by a designated communications or public relations representative, not the incident commander directly.
Wrong (D): Writing the post-incident report is a documentation task, not the incident commander''s primary coordination role during active response.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3706;

-- Q3707: Incident Response and Recovery [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Confirming that the server boots successfully and basic services start normally","Renaming the server to a new hostname before reconnecting to the network","Verifying the backup is clean, validating system integrity, and monitoring for compromise indicators","Updating the server''s visual configuration settings and desktop appearance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, Section 3.4, before returning a restored system to production, the team must verify the backup predates the compromise, validate system integrity, apply missing patches, and monitor for indicators of compromise during a controlled reintroduction period.
Wrong (A): A successful boot confirms basic functionality but does not verify the backup is free of attacker persistence mechanisms.
Wrong (B): Renaming the server is an administrative action that provides no security assurance about the restored system''s integrity.
Wrong (C): Updating desktop appearance is a cosmetic change with no relevance to verifying system integrity or security posture.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3707;

-- Q3708: Incident Response and Recovery [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["To rank employees by their seniority level within the organizational hierarchy","To manage employee vacation schedules and time-off rotation planning","To determine the physical placement of server racks in the data center","To define when and to whom incidents should be escalated based on severity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, Section 2.1, an escalation matrix defines conditions, severity thresholds, and notification timelines for routing incidents to appropriate decision-makers such as the CISO, legal counsel, or executive leadership.
Wrong (A): Employee seniority ranking is an HR function unrelated to the incident escalation criteria defined in an IR plan.
Wrong (B): Vacation scheduling has no connection to incident response; an escalation matrix addresses security event routing.
Wrong (C): Server rack placement is a data center operations concern, not a component of the incident response escalation framework.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3708;

-- Q3709: Incident Response and Recovery [hard/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Zero-day exploitation of a previously unknown software vulnerability","Phishing attacks delivered through deceptive email or messaging channels","Denial of service aimed at disrupting system availability for legitimate users","Living off the land using legitimate pre-installed tools to evade detection"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2 and the MITRE ATT&CK framework, living off the land (LOtL) techniques use legitimate tools already present — such as PowerShell, WMI, and RDP — for malicious purposes, evading signature-based detection.
Wrong (A): Zero-day exploitation targets unknown vulnerabilities in software, not the misuse of legitimate administrative tools.
Wrong (B): Phishing is a social engineering technique for initial access, not a method of lateral movement using existing tools.
Wrong (C): Denial of service disrupts availability through resource exhaustion, which is unrelated to using legitimate tools for lateral movement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3709;

-- Q3710: Incident Response and Recovery [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Containment actions like rebooting or patching can destroy volatile evidence and alter system state","Evidence preservation matters only for processing insurance claims after an incident","Preserving evidence before containment unnecessarily delays the overall response effort","There is no operational reason to preserve evidence before performing containment actions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, Section 3.3, volatile data such as RAM contents, active connections, and running processes is lost when systems are isolated or shut down. Capturing forensic images first preserves evidence needed for root cause analysis and legal proceedings.
Wrong (B): Evidence preservation supports investigation, legal proceedings, and regulatory compliance — not just insurance claims.
Wrong (C): NIST guidance explicitly recommends evidence capture before containment; the brief delay protects critical forensic artifacts.
Wrong (D): Volatile evidence is permanently lost once containment actions alter system state, making pre-containment preservation essential.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3710;

-- Q3714: Privacy Requirements and Data Protection [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Notify the supervisory authority within 72 hours if the breach risks individuals'' rights","No notification is required because no patient data was actually exfiltrated","Notify only the affected patients directly within a 24-hour notification window","Wait until the full investigation concludes before issuing any breach notifications"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under GDPR Article 33, the hospital must notify the supervisory authority within 72 hours of becoming aware of a breach likely to result in risk to individuals'' rights. Article 4(12) defines breaches to include unavailability of personal data, which the 36-hour encryption caused.
Wrong (B): GDPR Article 4(12) explicitly includes data unavailability as a breach type; exfiltration is not required to trigger notification obligations.
Wrong (C): Patient notification is governed by Article 34 and applies only when high risk exists; the 72-hour authority notification under Article 33 is the primary obligation.
Wrong (D): GDPR requires notification within 72 hours; waiting for investigation completion would likely violate this timeline requirement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3714;

-- Q3738: Privacy Requirements and Data Protection [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The accuracy principle requiring personal data to be kept correct and current","The storage limitation principle restricting how long data may be retained","The data protection by default principle requiring maximum privacy settings initially","The accountability principle requiring the controller to demonstrate compliance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): GDPR Article 25 requires that by default only personal data necessary for each processing purpose is collected. Settings must be configured to maximum privacy by default — users should not need to take action to protect their data from unnecessary sharing.
Wrong (A): The accuracy principle (Article 5(1)(d)) addresses data correctness, not default privacy settings for new user accounts.
Wrong (B): Storage limitation (Article 5(1)(e)) governs data retention periods, not the default sharing configuration presented to new users.
Wrong (D): Accountability (Article 5(2)) requires demonstrating compliance but does not specifically address default privacy settings for users.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3738;

-- Q3748: SOC Engagements [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Security and Availability, since uptime ensures processing and security protects cardholder data","Security and Confidentiality, since protecting cardholder data addresses the merchants'' primary concern","Security and Processing Integrity, since Processing Integrity addresses completeness, accuracy, and timeliness","All five trust services criteria, since financial transaction processing requires comprehensive coverage"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AICPA TSP Section 100, Security is mandatory in every SOC 2 engagement. Processing Integrity specifically evaluates whether system processing is complete, valid, accurate, timely, and authorized — directly addressing the merchants'' assurance requirement.
Wrong (A): Availability addresses system uptime but does not evaluate whether transactions are processed completely and accurately.
Wrong (B): Confidentiality protects sensitive data from unauthorized disclosure but does not address transaction processing accuracy or completeness.
Wrong (D): SOC 2 criteria are selected based on relevance; there is no requirement to include all five when only specific assurance needs exist.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3748;

-- Q3754: SOC Engagements [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The service auditor''s independent opinion on the operating effectiveness of controls","The user entity''s own assessment of its internal control environment","A written assertion on the fairness of the system description and control effectiveness","The listing of complementary user entity controls that clients must implement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320), management provides a written assertion that the system description is fairly presented and that controls are suitably designed and, for Type II, operated effectively throughout the period.
Wrong (A): The independent opinion is the service auditor''s responsibility, not management''s; management provides the assertion that the auditor evaluates.
Wrong (B): User entity controls are assessed by the user entities themselves, not provided by the service organization''s management.
Wrong (D): While complementary user entity controls are disclosed in the report, they represent assumptions about user responsibilities, not management''s written assertion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3754;

-- Q3756: SOC Engagements [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["To design and implement the service organization''s internal controls directly","To certify that no control deficiencies exist within the service organization","To prepare the system description document on behalf of service organization management","To opine on the system description''s fairness and the design and operating effectiveness of controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320.35), the service auditor expresses an opinion on whether the system description is fairly presented and controls are suitably designed and, for Type II, operated effectively during the period.
Wrong (A): Designing and implementing controls is management''s responsibility; the auditor provides independent assurance, not control design services.
Wrong (B): The auditor expresses an opinion, not a certification; the opinion may include identified exceptions or deficiencies.
Wrong (C): Management is responsible for preparing the system description; the auditor evaluates its fairness rather than authoring it.$EXPL$
